{"version":"6.9","hitCount":2,"request":{"queryString":"JOURNAL:\"Nucleic Acid Therapeutics\" AND (hydrocephalus OR ventriculomegaly OR \"choroid plexus\")","resultType":"core","cursorMark":"*","pageSize":40,"sort":"","synonym":false},"resultList":{"result":[{"id":"31821125","source":"MED","pmid":"31821125","pmcid":"PMC6987735","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC6987735"]},"doi":"10.1089/nat.2019.0796","title":"Nonclinical Safety Profile of Revusiran, a 1st-Generation GalNAc-siRNA Conjugate for Treatment of Hereditary Transthyretin-Mediated Amyloidosis.","authorString":"Sutherland JE, Hettinger JL, Chan A, Gilbert J, Warner GL, Davis WP.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Sutherland JE","firstName":"Jessica E","lastName":"Sutherland","initials":"JE","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Alnylam Pharmaceuticals, Inc., Cambridge, Massachusetts."}]}},{"fullName":"Hettinger JL","firstName":"Julia L","lastName":"Hettinger","initials":"JL","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Alnylam Pharmaceuticals, Inc., Cambridge, Massachusetts."}]}},{"fullName":"Chan A","firstName":"Amy","lastName":"Chan","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Alnylam Pharmaceuticals, Inc., Cambridge, Massachusetts."}]}},{"fullName":"Gilbert J","firstName":"Jason","lastName":"Gilbert","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Alnylam Pharmaceuticals, Inc., Cambridge, Massachusetts."}]}},{"fullName":"Warner GL","firstName":"Garvin L","lastName":"Warner","initials":"GL","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Alnylam Pharmaceuticals, Inc., Cambridge, Massachusetts."}]}},{"fullName":"Davis WP","firstName":"Wendell P","lastName":"Davis","initials":"WP","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Alnylam Pharmaceuticals, Inc., Cambridge, Massachusetts."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"30","journalIssueId":2905657,"dateOfPublication":"2020 Feb","monthOfPublication":2,"yearOfPublication":2020,"printPublicationDate":"2020-02-01","journal":{"title":"Nucleic acid therapeutics","medlineAbbreviation":"Nucleic Acid Ther","issn":"2159-3337","essn":"2159-3345","isoabbreviation":"Nucleic Acid Ther","nlmid":"101562758"}},"pubYear":"2020","pageInfo":"33-49","abstractText":"Revusiran is a 1st-generation short interfering RNA targeting transthyretin conjugated to an <i>N</i>-acetylgalactosamine ligand to facilitate delivery to hepatocytes via uptake by the asialoglycoprotein receptors. Revusiran, in development for the treatment of hereditary transthyretin-mediated amyloidosis, was discontinued after an imbalance in deaths in the \"ENDEAVOUR\" phase 3 clinical trial. Nonclinical safety assessments included safety pharmacology, acute and repeat-dose toxicity, genotoxicity, and carcinogenicity. There were no effects on cardiovascular or respiratory function in monkeys after single doses of up to 100 mg/kg. No neurological effects were noted in monkeys in repeat-dose studies up to 300 mg/kg. Revusiran was well tolerated in repeat-dose mouse (weekly doses) and rat and monkey (five daily doses followed by weekly doses) toxicity studies. The no observed adverse effect level (NOAEL) in rats was 30 mg/kg based on reversible microscopic changes in liver that were accompanied by correlating elevations in clinical chemistry at higher doses. Dose-limiting toxicity was absent in monkeys, and the NOAEL was 200 mg/kg. There was no evidence of genotoxicity <i>in vitro</i> or <i>in vivo</i> at limit doses or carcinogenicity in a 2-year study in rats at doses up to 100 mg/kg. Overall, these results demonstrate that revusiran had a favorable nonclinical safety profile.","affiliation":"Alnylam Pharmaceuticals, Inc., Cambridge, Massachusetts.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"